# SYNTAX TEST "text.html.tiddlywiki5" "Anything between a tag name and its close must parse as an attribute"

# html.js takes attributes in a loop and stops at the first one parseAttribute declines. It then
# demands a closing slash or a greater-than at that position, and returns null when it finds
# neither — so a single stretch of text that parses as no attribute refuses the WHOLE tag, and
# TiddlyWiki yields plain text for all of it.
#
# Most junk escapes this, because parseAttribute is generous: reAttributeName reads
# ([^\/\s>"'`=:]+) and an attribute needs no value, so <$link ###> binds ### as a valueless
# attribute and the tag stands. Three shapes measured against the parser do not escape it:
#
#   <$link to=<<>>>       a << with a >> ahead that names no macro. parseutils.js returns null
#                         deliberately here, its comment naming the intent: the enclosing tag
#                         fails rather than silently binding the attribute to true and reading the
#                         remainder as further attributes. Restores v5.3.8 behaviour.
#   <$link a/b="x">       a slash stands outside the attribute name class, and it reads as the
#                         self-closing slash instead, after which b="x" satisfies no closer.
#   <$link to: "x">       to: and a quote. The colon carries no assignment — TiddlyWiki binds
#                         valueless attributes here, and <$text text: word/> binds two of them —
#                         so the quote opens nothing the loop can take.
#
# The tag-name law next door narrows by a character class, which a begin pattern can express.
# This one narrows by whether a LATER region parses, over an unbounded attribute list where any
# value may itself carry quotes, braces, backticks or nested angle brackets. A begin pattern
# cannot look that far.
#
# Cost of leaving it: three shapes a wiki reaches mid-keystroke or by mistake, each of which
# colours as a working tag until the author renders it.

<$link to=<<>>>go</$link>
# ^^^^ - entity.name.tag.html.tiddlywiki5

<$link a/b="x">go</$link>
# ^^^^ - entity.name.tag.html.tiddlywiki5

<$link to: "x">go</$link>
# ^^^^ - entity.name.tag.html.tiddlywiki5
